WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:05.300
These are the heroes and demigods of modern
capitalist mythology.

00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:10.620
We tell and retell their biographies while
venerating their awe-inspiring works.

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:15.960
If there is such a thing as “winning”
the game of life, most people would say this is it.

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:21.780
Our media is fairly obsessed with entrepreneurs,
whether it’s Shark Tank inventors, celebrity

00:00:21.789 --> 00:00:26.669
restaurateurs, march-hawking bloggers, or
makeup tutorial moguls.

00:00:26.669 --> 00:00:32.250
We’re bombarded with messages that suggests
that entrepreneurship is the only path to

00:00:32.250 --> 00:00:34.300
real wealth and fulfillment.

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:39.260
Everyone feels the pressure to quit the day-job,
reach for the stars, and be your own boss!

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:43.310
But as any successful entrepreneur will tell
you, it’s not for everyone.

00:00:43.310 --> 00:00:48.460
The autonomy and independence can be fulfilling,
but you’ll probably be working longer hours

00:00:48.460 --> 00:00:50.710
for less money—at least at first.

00:00:50.710 --> 00:00:55.980
And having a great product idea or visionary
artistic talent is just one small element.

00:00:55.980 --> 00:01:00.720
Whether you want to be a freelance graphic
designer, an Instagram influencer, or the

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:05.600
inventor of a squirrel-shocking bird-feeder
(oh it’s a thing…look it up), most of

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:11.120
your time will be taken up with new tasks
and duties that you may or may not find enjoyable.

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:15.000
So before you quit your 9-to-5, let us show
you some of the many hats you’ll have to

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:18.000
wear if you want to be your own boss.

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:30.400
So, you think your cupcakes are the bomb and
decided to start your own business?

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:33.380
Congratulations, you’re not just a baker
anymore.

00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:35.460
Now you’re a CFO!

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:40.090
Cash is the lifeblood of any business and
no matter how much people like your product,

00:01:40.090 --> 00:01:45.040
if you’re not balancing revenue and expenses
correctly, your biz will bleed out.

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:50.240
The two main skills a CFO needs are Budgeting
and Accounting.

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:54.460
Budgeting is deciding how to spend your money
to get the most bang for you bucks.

00:01:54.460 --> 00:01:58.720
It means being realistic about what you have
to work with, and eliminating waste wherever

00:01:58.720 --> 00:01:59.720
possible.

00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:04.820
If you’ve never kept a personal home budget
you might want to start to get some practice.

00:02:04.820 --> 00:02:09.119
Accounting is minding your business’s financial
records, which includes tracking your income

00:02:09.119 --> 00:02:13.300
and costs, estimating your quarterly taxes,
and maybe even payroll.

00:02:13.300 --> 00:02:18.220
You’ll probably have to learn an accounting
program like Quickbooks, or maybe hire a bookkeeper

00:02:18.220 --> 00:02:19.400
to lighten the load.

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:23.780
But most entrepreneurs will tell you that
you’ll always have to be involved.

00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:26.670
No one will care about these numbers as much
as you do.

00:02:26.670 --> 00:02:31.660
In addition to being CFO, you are also now
the head of your company’s Human Resources

00:02:31.660 --> 00:02:32.500
department.

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:37.900
That means you’re in charge of hiring, firing,
training, delegating tasks and responsibilities,

00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:41.240
as well as figuring out payroll and benefits
for your staff.

00:02:41.240 --> 00:02:42.180
What’s that?

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:43.840
You don’t have a staff yet?

00:02:43.849 --> 00:02:46.370
Yes, you do: yourself!

00:02:46.370 --> 00:02:50.210
Leaving a salaried position means finding
your own health insurance, selecting your

00:02:50.210 --> 00:02:54.880
own retirement plan, and picking up the slack
for payroll taxes that you used to split with

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:55.880
your employer.

00:02:55.880 --> 00:03:01.040
When it is time to bring on employees, finding
talented and dedicated people—and keeping

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:02.820
them happy—is essential.

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:07.460
Many entrepreneurs credit their success with
being able to attract and retain employees

00:03:07.460 --> 00:03:09.160
smarter than themselves.

00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:13.819
There are lots of awesome books and podcasts
on assembling and managing teams, and we’ll

00:03:13.820 --> 00:03:15.940
make sure to share our favorites in the doobleydoo.

00:03:15.940 --> 00:03:20.480
As a supervisor, you’re not just a manager
of people, but also of time.

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:24.920
Time is money, as they say, and making sure
that neither you nor your employees are wasting

00:03:24.930 --> 00:03:26.260
it is crucial.

00:03:26.260 --> 00:03:30.350
Our friend Chelsea over at The Financial Diet
wanted to give her two cents on the biggest

00:03:30.350 --> 00:03:33.500
lessons she’s learned when it comes to managing
time.

00:03:33.500 --> 00:03:37.020
So here's what I did wrong when I first made the jump to being an entrepreneur.

00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:39.940
I tried to force a "normal" schedule.

00:03:39.940 --> 00:03:45.740
It made me feel like I was working harder if I forced myself to work into that 9-5 even if that wasn't

00:03:45.740 --> 00:03:47.460
when I was getting my best work done.

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:53.020
I would save the "easy" little things for last because often when I didn't have a fixed timeline throught my

00:03:53.020 --> 00:03:58.500
day that meant just pushing off the tasks that I didn't really want to do and felt like I could do later.

00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:03.580
And the third thing I did wrong was forcing myself to have bigger chunks of "offline" time.

00:04:03.580 --> 00:04:08.680
Essentially, taking weekends and vacations as the time when I would totally unplug.

00:04:08.680 --> 00:04:10.240
And this is how I fixed them:

00:04:10.240 --> 00:04:16.160
Instead of forcing myself to fit into a 9-5, I leaned into the times that I did my best work.

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:21.100
And in many cases, for me, being a night owl that meant getting work done at night when my brain was thinking

00:04:21.100 --> 00:04:22.140
its best.

00:04:22.140 --> 00:04:26.420
The fact that you're able to set your own schedule, as an entrepreneur or self-employed person, is something

00:04:26.420 --> 00:04:28.900
you should take advantage of not fight.

00:04:28.900 --> 00:04:33.780
Secondly, I started doing all those easy little things FIRST thing in the morning because I found that

00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:39.080
when i got them right out of the way I felt accomplished and that sense of accomplishment snowballed

00:04:39.080 --> 00:04:44.520
into being able to take care of bigger and bigger tasks. And plus all that little easy stuff didn't keep

00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:48.720
falling to the bottom or get kicked down to the never slot on my to-do list.

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:55.580
And lastly, I started spacing out my offline time giving myself dedicated chunks, even throughout my day,

00:04:55.580 --> 00:04:59.940
where I was away from my computer and specifically forcing myself not to work.

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:05.400
When you don't have dedicated work days and vacation days you can constantly be pressuring and guilting

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:08.100
yourself into getting more and more work done.

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:11.140
And ultimately that leaves you more drained and unproductive

00:05:11.140 --> 00:05:17.140
So by dedicating those offline times throughout my day and throughout my week I was often feeling refreshed

00:05:17.140 --> 00:05:20.620
and not like I had to force every minute into a work minute.

00:05:20.620 --> 00:05:24.660
The next hat you’ll wear is a big one: Marketing
Director.

00:05:24.660 --> 00:05:28.140
Attracting customers to your brand is an enormous
job!

00:05:28.140 --> 00:05:33.280
You’ve got to conduct market research, create
&amp; test advertising, design promotional materials,

00:05:33.280 --> 00:05:36.130
manage social media accounts and more!

00:05:36.130 --> 00:05:39.760
And no, a few random posts on Instagram aren’t
going to cut it.

00:05:39.760 --> 00:05:45.139
Marketing is a complicated, multi-layered
discipline that involves statistics, psychology

00:05:45.139 --> 00:05:46.160
and art.

00:05:46.160 --> 00:05:50.030
If you’re lucky enough to afford it, you
can hire a specialist to help you, though

00:05:50.030 --> 00:05:53.550
many small business owners have to figure
it out on their own at first.

00:05:53.550 --> 00:05:58.040
Luckily, there are plenty of free resources,
like this episode on Market Research we did

00:05:58.040 --> 00:05:59.040
a few weeks ago!

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:04.380
For many new entrepreneurs, the hardest realization
about marketing is that the job is never done!

00:06:04.380 --> 00:06:09.139
Even if your product is a stone cold hit,
you will always have to keep reminding people

00:06:09.139 --> 00:06:10.210
about it!

00:06:10.210 --> 00:06:12.570
Even Coca Cola still advertises.

00:06:12.570 --> 00:06:16.949
Once your head of marketing has attracted
a bunch of potential customers, it’s time

00:06:16.949 --> 00:06:19.380
for your Salesperson to seal the deal.

00:06:19.380 --> 00:06:20.750
(That’s you again.)

00:06:20.750 --> 00:06:25.720
If marketing is your public image, sales is
your personal relationships with customers.

00:06:25.720 --> 00:06:31.540
Being a good salesperson doesn’t come by
intuition, but by honing and practicing skills,

00:06:31.550 --> 00:06:35.600
like active listening or networking effectively
in a room full of strangers.

00:06:35.600 --> 00:06:40.419
You’ll also need to learn how to roll with
rejection without taking it personally, and

00:06:40.419 --> 00:06:45.570
how to create a repeatable step by step process
that nurtures potential customers all the

00:06:45.570 --> 00:06:48.760
way from initial interest to the sale and
beyond.

00:06:48.760 --> 00:06:50.020
And finally there’s —

00:06:50.020 --> 00:06:51.420
— wait…”finally!?”

00:06:51.420 --> 00:06:53.020
There’s so much more we haven’t talked
about!

00:06:53.020 --> 00:06:55.320
There’s logistics, customer service, legal!

00:06:55.320 --> 00:06:59.600
Not to mention hard-to-nail down “soft skills”
like keeping yourself motivated and dealing

00:06:59.610 --> 00:07:00.640
with irregular income.

00:07:00.640 --> 00:07:04.919
I know, I know, but while we don’t want
them to have rose-colored glasses, we don’t

00:07:04.919 --> 00:07:06.900
want to totally scare them off!

00:07:06.900 --> 00:07:12.169
Honestly, so much of this can and will come
with time and experience if you have the fortitude

00:07:12.169 --> 00:07:12.660
and patience.

00:07:12.660 --> 00:07:16.340
And if this doesn’t sound like it’s for
you, that’s okay!

00:07:16.350 --> 00:07:21.260
For many people, having a solid day-job that
they enjoy can be more fulfilling—and less

00:07:21.260 --> 00:07:24.540
stressful—than wearing all these hats at
once.

00:07:24.540 --> 00:07:26.020
And that’s our two cents!

00:07:26.020 --> 00:07:29.080
Thanks to Chelsea for contributing to this
episode.

00:07:29.080 --> 00:07:34.700
For more fresh takes on personal finance you
can subscribe to the Financial Diet on YouTube.

00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:39.740
If you've started your own business what was the best piece of advice you received?

00:07:39.740 --> 00:07:41.780
Be sure to share it with us in the comments.

